Docker named volumes maintain database records independently of the container lifecycle.

The database container can be recreated without deleting the persistent storage volume.

## Data Persistence Verification

Access the PostgreSQL database:

```powershell
docker exec -it library-db psql -U loanuser -d librarydb
```

Create a sample table and insert a fictional library loan record:

```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS loans (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    book_title VARCHAR(100),
    borrower VARCHAR(100)
);

INSERT INTO loans (book_title, borrower)
VALUES ('Container Security Fundamentals', 'Test Borrower');

SELECT id, book_title, borrower FROM loans;
```

Exit PostgreSQL and recreate the database container without deleting its volume:

```powershell
docker compose up -d --force-recreate database
docker exec library-db pg_isready -U loanuser -d librarydb
```

After PostgreSQL is ready, verify the original database record:

```powershell
docker exec library-db psql -U loanuser -d librarydb -c "SELECT id, book_title, borrower FROM loans;"
```

## Expected Verification Results

| Verification Test | Expected Result |
|---|---|
| Named volume creation | library-data exists |
| Database record insertion | Successful |
